-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Downstream Implications
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+**Tablet-sync must**:
+- Retain the base revision ID with each locally drafted edit so submissions carry the correct base
+- Handle fetching a visit that returns no current revision (competing state) by showing the worker that their submission is under review rather than displaying a nonexistent current state
+- Display when other workers have submitted competing revisions to the same visit the current worker has edited
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+**Review-export must**:
+- Provide a comparison interface showing competing revision payloads side-by-side with differences highlighted
+- Exclude visits in competing state from export eligibility until resolved
+- Support coordinators creating a new resolution revision (not just selecting an existing one) when neither competing revision is fully correct
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions sent to manual review | Field-aware three-way merge: automatic combination of disjoint field changes, with overlapping changes sent to review | Accept larger review queues to avoid merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits without noticing the relationship. Example: a worker corrects a species identification from Species A to Species B, and another worker updates Species A's cover estimate - these are disjoint fields but scientifically coupled. Manual review of whole visits preserves the worker's submitted observation as a complete unit and surfaces these relationships. Field-aware merging would remain credible if the team later finds manual review volume unsustainable and is willing to invest in maintained merge rules plus field-level provenance in the export. The coordinators' scientific expertise is the current merge engine. |
+| Revision identity | Server-assigned revision ID on acceptance | Client-assigned revision ID in submission | Server assignment ensures uniqueness and prevents submission-time collisions. The server controls the revision sequence and can guarantee monotonic ordering for a visit. |
+| Resolution can create new revision | Resolution operation accepts either selecting an existing revision or creating a new one | Resolution must select one of the competing revisions | Coordinators need the ability to combine aspects of competing revisions or apply their own correction when reviewing. Requiring selection of an existing revision would force them to pick one submission and then immediately submit another edit, creating unnecessary round-trips and audit confusion. |
